description:
## Background

The transaction engine's promises are what make omaddons safe to hand to an agent:
- every folder is wholly old or wholly new across crashes (vision §5.5 Invariant, D25);
- the next change command recovers an interrupted run;
- "SavedVariables are never written while the client runs, under any policy or flag" (README Safety model);
- undo brings SavedVariables back.

The post-M2 audit of 2026-10-16, with re-verification and reproductions on 2026-10-18, found that these hold on the happy path but break in the failure paths the journal exists for:
- a failed in-process recovery deletes the journal and the stage dir holding the old folder;
- undo half-applies when WTF/ is symlinked out of the client;
- recovery rolls an undo forward and writes SavedVariables while the game runs;
- journal step identity uses st_dev, which btrfs and FUSE renumber after a reboot;
- running detection fails open when /proc is unreadable;
- a torn history line swallows the next transaction;
- retention can prune the snapshot a deferred restore needs;
- lowercase wtf/ and account/ directories are skipped silently;
- a leftover journal on one client blocks every other client;
- several lock holders never recover;
- a roll-forward misreports atomic_swap.

## Goals

- No failure path deletes data the journal or a snapshot still needs.
- Recovery after any crash, reboot or double fault either completes, or keeps everything for the next lock holder and says so.
- The SavedVariables rule ("never written while the client runs") holds in every code path, recovery and unknown-running-state included.

## Success Criteria

- Every child bead is closed, each with its reproduction turned into a regression test.
- The crash matrix stays green, and the gaps its sibling bead in the engine-tests epic adds (failed recovery, mid-swap, undo, SavedVariables) pass.
- `argc gate` passes; README Safety model and the vision bead record every behaviour or contract change.

## Non-Goals

- No `doctor` (M4): a journal that still needs manual repair stays reported as `journal_pending`, now limited to its own target.
- No change to the swap mechanism itself (`RENAME_EXCHANGE`/`NOREPLACE` with the two-rename fallback, D25).

## Considerations

- Several children change `recover_all`/`recover_tx` and the error branch after a failed commit (`src/engine/journal.rs`, `src/engine/tx.rs`, `src/engine/undo.rs`). Their `blocks` edges give an order. The recommended shape is one `RecoveryEnv` struct carrying the running predicate and the per-journal scopes, so the signature changes once.
- The order matters for safety: the bead that defers SavedVariables during recovery lands before the bead that keeps failed journals, or a deterministic restore failure would block every later command.

## Background

Network privacy and download integrity are product promises:
- README TL;DR: "every download checked against its MD5";
- Network and privacy: a closed host allowlist, every request and git run in `net.log`, and an absolute `--offline` (vision G5, D12, D13).

The 2026-10-16 post-M2 audit, re-verified with reproductions on 2026-10-18, found places where the code does not fully keep them:
- WoWInterface and Tukui downloads without a usable MD5 install unverified, silently;
- the git and HTTP allowlists leak into each other;
- `--offline` still runs a credential command;
- git receives the real HOME, so libcurl can send `.netrc` credentials;
- a git run succeeds when its `net.log` line cannot be written;
- git clones have no byte cap;
- mid-body failures are never retried and are misreported as io errors on a temp file.

It also found ref-parsing and selection bugs:
- pasted release links lose their pin for encoded or slash tags;
- a github.com link without a repository becomes a `url:` ref;
- catalog families filter dependencies though documented as restricting nothing;
- Tukui's patch list waives a rule only release.json may waive;
- a failed WoWInterface index fetch never makes `search` partial.

## Goals

- Every README statement in "Sources and refs" and "Network and privacy" is true of the code.
- No network contact, credential use or download escapes the allowlist, the log, `--offline` or the size limits.

## Success Criteria

- Every child bead is closed with a regression test (replay fixtures; no live network in the gate).
- `argc gate` passes; README and the vision bead record each contract change (new warning codes, stricter allowlists).

## Non-Goals

- No CurseForge, Wago or GraphQL work (M5).

## Considerations

- Four children edit `GitRunner`'s command construction, `run` and `finish` (`src/sources/git_hosts.rs`): land them one after another.
- Two children add a field to `Asset`, which touches about 21 struct literals; coordinate.

## Background

"The game's rules decide" and "detection is data" are design principles 1 and 5 in README (vision D5, D15, D29). The 2026-10-16 post-M2 audit, re-verified with reproductions on 2026-10-18, found gaps in discovery, ClientRules and the TOC engine:
- the TOC parser misses the 12.0.7 end-of-line directive conditionals that ElvUI ships, keeps the first of repeated directives where the client keeps the last, and drops repeated `Dependencies` lines;
- three ClientRules fields are read by nothing;
- a family override on a client with no readable version picks the fallback rules;
- custom 1.12-based clients stamped 1.17/1.18 (Turtle WoW) are read as Classic Era;
- the product.db reader takes an install's uid as its product code;
- the Bottles reader misses moved bottles directories and custom-path bottles;
- a client found only while it ran is lost after it exits;
- unknown `[rules]` keys are ignored silently;
- a dead discovery mode remains.

It also found a gap in target identity: `install` and `adopt` record folders twice when clients share one AddOns directory, and a remove through the other client then deletes them without a warning.

## Goals

- Every rule omaddons applies to an archive matches what the real client does, as far as public evidence shows.
- Every documented override and config key does what README says.
- Two clients sharing one AddOns directory can never both own a folder.

## Success Criteria

- Every child bead is closed with fixtures and regression tests.
- `argc gate` passes; README and the vision bead record each rules-table or contract change.

## Non-Goals

- No `doctor` finding for shared AddOns directories (M4); this epic only prevents double ownership.
- No new families (closed enum, README design principle 5 as narrowed by the README-accuracy bead).

## Considerations

- The conditional-directive rewrite depends on the ClientRules field cleanup.
- Real-file confirmation of the Turtle WoW stamp lives in the real-world verification epic, deferred on the owner.

## Background

"The CLI is the product" (README design principle 4; vision D17, D19, D31). Agents are told to run `fix_argv`, `alternatives[].argv` and `commands[].argv` as given, and to trust one JSON envelope on stdout. The 2026-10-16 post-M2 audit, re-verified with reproductions on 2026-10-18, found contract holes.

The worst: `omaddons uninstall dbm` answers with a `fix_argv` of `omaddons install dbm`, the opposite verb, which an obedient agent would run.

The others:
- failure envelopes drop every warning (a `journal_pending` is lost);
- unparsable JSON flag spellings get no envelope;
- the panic path can print two envelopes, none, or a malformed one;
- some fixes point back at the failing command, or are commands written as prose;
- state-changing commands outside the engine record no history;
- `auth github set --dry-run` claims it stored the token.

And UX defects:
- `search` shows raw HTML entities and terminal control characters, ranks unrelated GitHub repositories first, and picks install hints by target id;
- `info` lists clients the release cannot load on;
- an `http://` fix leads straight into `host_blocked`;
- dry-run text reads as past tense;
- tables misalign;
- `net-log` has no limit;
- the no-op handling of `--dry-run`/`--reason` on read commands is undocumented.

## Goals

- No suggestion the CLI makes can name a different mutating command than the one the user meant, and every suggested argv parses.
- Exactly one well-formed envelope on stdout under `--json`, with every warning the run raised, whatever happens.
- Every state change is attributed in history.

## Success Criteria

- Every child bead is closed; the contract suite parses every suggested argv and covers a corpus of wrong invocations.
- `argc gate` passes; README and the vision bead record each contract change (aliases, new warnings, data keys).

## Non-Goals

- The full M4 intent inference (misplaced target names), `capabilities`, `robot-docs` and the wire-name golden file. The usage-fix bead pulls only vision §5.10's verb aliases forward, extending M2-D39, because that is the structural fix for the `uninstall`→`install` inversion.

## Considerations

- The contract-suite bead depends on the usage-fix bead, and the panic-path bead on the warnings bead.
- Several children change README's command reference and contract tables; the README-accuracy bead owns the code-table rows and counts.

## Background

Several facts the safety model rests on have only ever been checked against synthetic fixtures (vision §4.4, §4.8 and §8 mark them UNVERIFIED; the M1 checkpoint comment says "Not verified on real installs: Wine process naming under Proton while the game runs, and Lutris, Heroic, Bottles and Flatpak layouts"). The 2026-10-16 post-M2 audit listed these gaps:
- no real client has been observed loading an addon omaddons installed (Forever is covered by omad-classic-forever-gaps-xud.6);
- Proton/umu process naming has never been observed with a client running, and running detection depends on it;
- the launcher readers have never been checked against upstream sources;
- four of the five legacy client versions exist only as PE stubs;
- 3.3.5a's `/reload` behaviour and comma-list reading are assumed;
- Turtle WoW's exe stamp is unconfirmed;
- keyless coverage of popular addons has never been measured.

## Goals

- Every UNVERIFIED assumption that a safety rule depends on is observed on a real client or confirmed from upstream source, and pinned in a regression fixture.

## Success Criteria

- Every child bead is closed with its evidence recorded (placeholders only, no personal data) and a vision comment for each confirmed or corrected fact.

## Non-Goals

- No code change unless the evidence shows a rule is wrong; such a fix gets its own bug bead (`--deps discovered-from:<the verification bead>`).

## Considerations

- Some children wait on things no agent can supply: a client running (the Forever stress test from 2026-10-19), play sessions on the owner's accounts, or client files only the owner has. They are deferred with the exact ask.
- Agents must never download Blizzard client binaries, and never write under any `WTF/`.

## Background

M2's end-to-end tests need fixtures M1 never needed:
- several releases of one repo over time (update, outdated, pins);
- an installed tree that is one version behind (`outdated`, `update`, adoption's `identified` tier);
- mutations of managed files (drift);
- readers for records, the files index, history and `net.log` (carry-over, records-only transactions, request economy);
- a release graph where addon A requires B (dependencies).

What exists today in `tests/common/` (DEC-8: synthetic trees, archives and HTTP fixture builders live there):
- `http.rs`: `fixture`, `fixture_bytes`, `gh_release`, `paths`, `replay`, `record`, `scenario`.
- `home.rs`: `Home::new`, `client`, `addons`, `state`, `scenario`, `retail_running`, `nothing_running`, `run`, `exec`, `exec_with`, `spawn`.
- `netlog.rs`: `read_net_log`, `count_host`, `assert_no_github_rest`, `assert_each_url_once`.
- `engine.rs`: the crash `Scene`.
- `trees.rs`: `BattlenetRoot`, `CustomRoot`, `tree_hash`.

Three facts limit them:
- **One fixture per request.** The replay loader refuses two fixtures with the same method and URL (`src/net/replay.rs:139`, "a second fixture for …"). `gh_release` names its `releases/latest` fixture `latest-<slug>-<tag>` (`tests/common/http.rs:67-77`), so calling it twice for one repo in one scenario fails to load. A second release of a repo cannot be added today.
- **Sequences are per process.** A fixture's responses are "consumed in order with the last repeating" (`replay.rs:6-7`), with the position kept in memory (`replay.rs:96`). Every CLI invocation starts the sequence again, so a sequence cannot span two commands.
- **Scenario switching is the cross-command tool.** Scenarios that span several commands switch fixture sets with `home.scenario(...)`, as `tests/install.rs:184` does to publish v2 after installing v1.

Building these helpers once keeps every feature bead's tests short and consistent. Seven beads wait on them. They serve the M2 goals "current by default" (request-economy assertions from `net.log`, not timing) and "no regression of M1 guarantees" (state assertions through the library's own readers).

Line numbers are as of commit `e902d36` (current `main`); the symbol names are what matter.

## Technical Approach

**Files:** `tests/common/http.rs`, `tests/common/home.rs`, `tests/common/netlog.rs` (exists; extend it), new `tests/common/drift.rs`, new `tests/common/graph.rs`, new `tests/common/state.rs`, `tests/common/mod.rs` (declare the new modules), `tests/common_selftest.rs`. `tests/common/engine.rs` changes only if its private readers (`Scene::generation`, `Scene::history`) move onto the new state readers.

The signatures below are the contract other beads code against. Parameter types may be adjusted while implementing, but names and behaviour stay.

### HTTP fixtures (`tests/common/http.rs`)

```rust
/// One response of a fixture sequence.
pub struct Resp { pub status: u16, pub headers: Vec<(String, String)>, pub body: Option<Vec<u8>> }
impl Resp {
    pub fn ok(body: impl Into<Vec<u8>>) -> Self;        // 200 with a body
    pub fn status(code: u16) -> Self;                    // any status, empty body
    pub fn header(self, name: &str, value: &str) -> Self;
}
/// A multi-response fixture for a GET of `url`: served in order, the last repeating.
pub fn fixture_seq(scenario: &Path, name: &str, url: &str, responses: &[Resp]);
/// `<base>/releases.atom` with one entry per (tag, release-notes HTML), newest first,
/// each with `<content type="html">` (escaped HTML) as GitHub serves it. With `etag`,
/// the first response carries that ETag and later ones are 304.
pub fn gh_feed(scenario: &Path, base: &str, entries: &[(&str, &str)], etag: Option<&str>);
/// Release `tag`: its release.json and asset zips, without touching `releases/latest`,
/// so several tags of one repo coexist in one scenario (also what `--version` fetches).
pub fn gh_release_v(scenario: &Path, base: &str, tag: &str, assets: &[Asset<'_>]);
/// The one `releases/latest` 302 fixture of the repo, pointing at `tag`. It replaces an
/// earlier one, including the `latest-<slug>-<tag>` file `gh_release` wrote.
pub fn gh_latest(scenario: &Path, base: &str, tag: &str);
```

- **`fixture_seq` is single-process only:** replay restarts every sequence in each CLI process (`replay.rs:6-7`). It suits in-process tests (`replay(env, scenario)` over one `Http`) and one-command scenarios such as "200 with an ETag, then 304" or "403, then 200". Multi-command scenarios switch fixture sets with `home.scenario(...)`.
- `gh_feed`'s entry shape follows the decoder's own sample of GitHub's `releases.atom` (the `RELEASES` test constant at `src/sources/atom.rs:218`): `id` is `tag:github.com,2008:Repository/<n>/<tag>`, `title` is the tag, `link` points at `releases/tag/<tag>`, plus `updated`. D6 (changelogs) and B2 (conditional feeds) use it.
- Keep `gh_release` as it is (single tag, latest included) for M1 tests. It equals `gh_release_v` plus `gh_latest`.

### Homes (`tests/common/home.rs`)

```rust
impl Home {
    /// Installs `old` (from `base`, e.g. `https://github.com/o/r`, as `gh:o/r`) on each
    /// target id with the binary, then switches to a fresh scenario where both tags exist
    /// and `releases/latest` points at `new`. Returns that scenario's directory.
    pub fn installed_behind(&mut self, base: &str, old: (&str, &[Asset<'_>]),
                            new: (&str, &[Asset<'_>]), targets: &[&str]) -> PathBuf;
    /// Builds a custom client under this home's tempdir and registers it with
    /// `omaddons targets add <path>`; returns its client dir.
    pub fn with_custom(&mut self, root: CustomRoot) -> PathBuf;
}
```

`installed_behind` asserts every install exited 0. `with_custom` is how a test gets two clients of one family (a Battle.net Classic Era client plus a custom vanilla client) for `-t vanilla` fan-out. `Home::seed_memo` belongs to B1 (`omad-m2-updates-adoption-2hp.2.1`), which defines the memo format.

### Drift mutators (`tests/common/drift.rs`)

```rust
pub fn edit_managed(file: &Path, contents: &[u8]);          // different bytes at the same path
pub fn delete_managed(path: &Path);                          // a file, or a whole folder
pub fn add_stray_file(folder: &Path, rel: &str, contents: &[u8]);
pub fn touch_identical(file: &Path);                         // same bytes, mtime moved >= 1 s forward
```

`touch_identical` rewrites the identical bytes and sets the mtime forward with `File::set_modified`, so a stat-first drift check must re-hash the file and find it clean. E2 (the drift detector) crafts its racy-window case (same size and mtime, different bytes) itself with `utimensat`.

### State readers (`tests/common/state.rs`)

```rust
pub fn key_of(home: &Home, target_id: &str) -> TargetKey;     // via load_registry
pub fn record(home: &Home, target_id: &str) -> TargetRecord;  // via load_record
pub fn files_index(home: &Home, target_id: &str) -> FilesIndex; // via store::load::<FilesIndex>
pub fn history(home: &Home) -> Vec<HistoryEntry>;             // via history::read, torn lines skipped
```

They load through the library's own readers (`omaddons::state::registry::load_registry`, `state::records::load_record`, `state::store::load`, `state::history::read`), so a format change breaks the tests loudly instead of being parsed around. Paths come from `http::paths(&home.env)`.

### `net.log` (`tests/common/netlog.rs`, extended)

```rust
pub fn net_log(home: &Home) -> NetLogView;
pub struct NetLogView { pub entries: Vec<NetLogEntry> }
impl NetLogView {
    pub fn count(&self, host: &str, purpose: &str) -> usize;   // e.g. ("github.com", "release-latest")
    pub fn count_purpose(&self, purpose: &str) -> usize;       // e.g. "asset"
    pub fn len(&self) -> usize;
}
```

The plan called the type `NetLog`. That name is already taken by `omaddons::net::NetLog`, which this file imports, so the view gets its own name. Purposes in use today include `release-latest`, `release-json` and `asset`.

### Release graphs (`tests/common/graph.rs`)

```rust
pub struct Node<'a> { pub base: &'a str, pub tag: &'a str, pub folders: &'a [Folder<'a>] }
pub struct Folder<'a> { pub name: &'a str, pub interface: &'a str,
                        pub deps: &'a [&'a str], pub optional: &'a [&'a str] }
/// One GitHub release per node (gh_release_v + gh_latest), each zip holding its folders
/// with `## Interface`, `## Version: <tag>`, `## Dependencies: …` and `## OptionalDeps: …`.
pub fn release_graph(scenario: &Path, nodes: &[Node<'_>]);
```

Graphs express chains (A → B → C), cycles (A ↔ B), in-archive requirements (A requires A_Lib in the same zip) and bridge folders (a second folder requiring an addon that is not in the graph). C4.1 adds its own test catalog override that maps a folder to a node's ref.

WoWI and Tukui fixture builders are written in D2, D3 and D4, next to their decoders.

## Acceptance Criteria

- Every helper above has a selftest in `tests/common_selftest.rs`:
  - `fixture_seq` serves its responses in order with the last repeating, within one process;
  - two tags of one repo load in one scenario through `gh_release_v` plus `gh_latest`, and `gh_latest` re-points `releases/latest`;
  - `gh_feed` decodes through the production Atom decoder and answers a conditional request with 304;
  - `installed_behind` leaves the old version recorded while the scenario's latest is the new one;
  - each drift mutator changes exactly what it says (`touch_identical` keeps the bytes and moves the mtime);
  - the state readers return the committed record, index and history;
  - `NetLogView` counts by host and purpose;
  - `release_graph` TOCs parse with the declared dependencies;
  - `with_custom` adds a discovered target.
- No helper writes outside its tempdir: the existing guard test `the_binary_never_touches_the_real_home` still passes.
- Existing users of `gh_release`, `read_net_log` and `Home` are unchanged.

## Test Plan

- The selftests themselves, in `tests/common_selftest.rs`, run in the normal gate.
- Each selftest that runs the binary uses `Home` and asserts the one-envelope contract through `Run::expect`.

## Considerations

- Keep builders deterministic: the same calls produce byte-identical trees and fixtures (the module rule in `tests/common/mod.rs`).
- Tests may write files directly: the single-writer rule (`tests/single_writer.rs`) covers `src/` only.
- Do not add a clock override seam. Time-dependent tests pre-seed `fetched_at` (B1's `seed_memo`) or pre-age files.
- These helpers are shared by beads that run in parallel. Changing a signature after it lands means updating every caller in the same commit.

**Common rules for every M2 bead.**
- `argc gate` passes before any commit that changes code, fixtures, snapshots or embedded assets (vision D16: a local gate, no CI). After adding a crate, run `argc gate --all` (cargo deny). Run `ubs` on the changed files.
- Every filesystem mutation goes through `src/fsx/` (enforced by `tests/single_writer.rs`). Commands never print: they return an `Outcome` rendered by `src/output/`. No `unwrap`/`expect` outside tests and no `unsafe`.
- New constants live in `src/politeness.rs` or `src/limits.rs` with a `REGISTRY` row.
- Every struct that reaches the wire, state files or plans derives `Serialize + JsonSchema`, field order is wire order, and maps are `BTreeMap` (DEC-3). Each command adds its own clap subcommand and flags only together with their behaviour (DEC-1: no stubs). New commands join a help group (A1) and emit progress events (A9) when they need more than one network round trip.
- Every error carries a `fix`, plus `fix_argv` when the fix is a command. Text-only fixes are listed in `TEXT_FIXES` in `tests/contract/main.rs`.
- New wire names (codes, keys, kinds, enum spellings) and new or changed constants are listed in the close comment, so H6 can record them on the vision bead (DEC-4, D21, D22). New or changed snapshots are reviewed, not blindly accepted.
- Test-only hooks (`OMADDONS_FAILPOINTS`, `OMADDONS_HTTP_FIXTURES`, `OMADDONS_RECORD`, `OMADDONS_PROC_ROOT`, `OMADDONS_LIVE`, `OMADDONS_REAL_MACHINE`) work in debug builds only (DEC-7). Fixture builders live in `tests/common/` (DEC-8).
- Work on `main`. Claim with `br update <id> --claim --transition-comment "claim: <files>"`. Treat unrecognised working-tree changes as a peer's work. No script-based code edits. File work found along the way with `--deps discovered-from:<id>`. Commit code and `.beads/` together after `br sync --flush-only`.

## Dependencies

Blocked by: none (can start immediately).

Unblocks:
- B2 `omad-m2-updates-adoption-2hp.2.2` (GitHub tag-first probe and conditional feeds): `gh_release_v`/`gh_latest` for changed and unchanged tags, `gh_feed` with an ETag for the 304 path, `NetLogView` for exact request counts.
- B5 `omad-m2-updates-adoption-2hp.2.5` (The outdated command): `installed_behind` across retail, era and classic, plus `net.log` counts.
- B6 `omad-m2-updates-adoption-2hp.2.6` (The update command): `installed_behind`, state readers and `net.log` counts.
- C4.1 `omad-m2-updates-adoption-2hp.3.4.1` (Dependency rounds on install): `release_graph` for chains, cycles, optional dependencies and depth limits.
- D4 `omad-m2-updates-adoption-2hp.4.4` (The Tukui source): `fixture_seq` for the token-rotation fixture, where the old token answers 403.
- E2 `omad-m2-updates-adoption-2hp.5.2` (The drift detector): the drift mutators.
- E5.1 `omad-m2-updates-adoption-2hp.5.5.1` (adopt report mode): `installed_behind` for the `identified` tier, and state readers.
- F3.1 `omad-m2-updates-adoption-2hp.6.3.1` (sync core): state readers and `net.log` counts for the zero-request re-run.

